## Exhaustive Matching
With sealed traits, the compiler warns about missing cases:
```scala
sealed trait Shape
case class Circle(r: Double) extends Shape
case class Square(s: Double) extends Shape

def area(shape: Shape): Double = shape match {
  case Circle(r) => math.Pi * r * r
  case Square(s) => s * s
  // compiler warns if a case is missing
}
```

## Guards in Pattern Matching
```scala
x match {
  case n: Int if n > 0 => "positive"
  case n: Int if n < 0 => "negative"
  case _ => "zero"
}
```

# Scala Traits, Abstract Classes, and Type Classes

## Overview
Scala uses traits (similar to Python's ABC/Protocol) and type classes for polymorphism.

## Traits (replacing Python Protocols and ABCs)

### Python Protocol → Scala Trait
```python
# Python
@runtime_checkable
class Tokenizable(Protocol):
    def to_token(self) -> str: ...
```

```scala
// Scala
trait Tokenizable {
  def toToken: String
}
```

### Abstract Base Class → Abstract Class or Trait
```python
# Python
class BaseTokenizer(ABC, Generic[T]):
    @abstractmethod
    def tokenize(self, value: T) -> Token: ...

    def tokenize_batch(self, values: Iterable[T]) -> Iterator[Token]:
        for v in values:
            yield self.tokenize(v)
```

```scala
// Scala - trait with abstract and concrete methods
trait BaseTokenizer[T] {
  def tokenize(value: T): Token  // abstract

  def tokenizeBatch(values: Iterable[T]): Iterator[Token] =
    values.iterator.map(tokenize)
}
```

## Type Classes (advanced polymorphism pattern)
Type classes provide ad-hoc polymorphism - adding behavior to types without modifying them:

```scala
// Define the type class
trait Tokenizer[A] {
  def tokenize(value: A): Token
}

// Provide instances
object Tokenizer {
  implicit val stringTokenizer: Tokenizer[String] = new Tokenizer[String] {
    def tokenize(value: String): Token = Token(value, TokenType.StringType)
  }

  implicit val intTokenizer: Tokenizer[Int] = new Tokenizer[Int] {
    def tokenize(value: Int): Token = Token(value.toString, TokenType.Numeric)
  }
}

// Use with context bounds
def process[A: Tokenizer](value: A): Token =
  implicitly[Tokenizer[A]].tokenize(value)
```

## Builder Pattern in Scala
The fluent builder pattern works naturally with method chaining:

```scala
class TokenizerBuilder[T] {
  private var normalizers: List[String => String] = Nil

  def withNormalizer(f: String => String): TokenizerBuilder[T] = {
    normalizers = normalizers :+ f
    this
  }

  def build(): T => Token = { value =>
    var str = value.toString
    normalizers.foreach(n => str = n(str))
    Token(str, TokenType.StringType)
  }
}
```

